.TH HD-RESTORE-VM 8 "October 2026" "hd-backup" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-restore-vm \- restore a virtual machine from a backup of hd-backup-vm
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-restore-vm
.I backup_dir
.RI [ new_name ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-restore-vm
restores a virtual machine from its backup folder
.IR backup_dir ,
the folder
.IR dir / vm
of
.BR hd\-backup\-vm (8).
The name of the folder is the name of the VM in the backup.
Run it as root.
.PP
It restores the disks in the XML of the last finished backup; streams of
disks that were removed from the VM are left alone.
For each disk, it asks for the ZFS dataset to restore into; the default is
the dataset of the original VM.
Each stream of the disk is received in order with
.BR "zfs receive \-F" ,
which overwrites a dataset that already exists.
.PP
It asks whether the network interfaces get new MAC addresses, so a copy does
not clash with the original VM on the network; the default keeps them.
.PP
The VM is defined in libvirt with the XML of the backup, with the disks
pointing at the datasets they were restored into, and the NVRAM of the backup
copied in place.
With
.IR new_name ,
the VM gets that name, a new UUID, and an NVRAM file of its own,
.IB new_name _VARS.fd
next to the one of the original VM.
The VM is not started.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the VM was restored, 1 on an error, 2 on wrong arguments, 130 when it
was interrupted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd\-backup\-vm (8),
.BR virsh (1),
.BR zfs (8)
